#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:4
msgid "Get Started"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:10
msgid "Install ``pytest``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:12
msgid ""
"Install ``pytest`` into a virtual environment, not into the Python "
"installation of your operating system (see :ref:`install-package-with-pip`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:15
msgid ""
"Create a virtual environment and install ``pytest`` into it, using the tool "
"of your choice:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:20
msgid ""
":mod:`venv` and :doc:`pip <pip:index>` ship with Python. Some Linux "
"distributions package them separately (for example ``python3-venv`` on "
"Debian and Ubuntu)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:24
msgid ""
"python3 -m venv .venv\n"
"source .venv/bin/activate   # on Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate\n"
"pip install -U pytest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:32
msgid ""
"`uv <https://docs.astral.sh/uv/>`__ creates and manages the virtual "
"environment of your project for you. In a directory with a "
"``pyproject.toml`` (``uv init`` creates one):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:36
msgid "uv add --dev pytest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:40
msgid ""
"Prefix the ``pytest`` commands on this page with ``uv run``, for example "
"``uv run pytest``, to run them inside the project environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:45
msgid ""
":doc:`virtualenv <virtualenv:index>` is a faster, more configurable "
"alternative to :mod:`venv`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:48
msgid ""
"virtualenv .venv\n"
"source .venv/bin/activate   # on Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate\n"
"pip install -U pytest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:54
msgid "Check that you installed the correct version:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:56
msgid ""
"$ pytest --version\n"
"pytest 9.2.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:61
msgid ""
"See :ref:`install-package-with-pip` for how to install the project you are "
"testing into the same environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:67
msgid "Create your first test"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:69
msgid ""
"Create a new file called ``test_sample.py``, containing a function, and a "
"test:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:71
msgid ""
"# content of test_sample.py\n"
"def func(x):\n"
"    return x + 1\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def test_answer():\n"
"    assert func(3) == 5"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:81
msgid "Run the test:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:83
msgid ""
"$ pytest\n"
"=========================== test session starts "
"============================\n"
"platform linux -- Python 3.x.y, pytest-9.x.y, pluggy-1.x.y\n"
"rootdir: /home/sweet/project\n"
"collected 1 item\n"
"\n"
"test_sample.py F                                                     [100%]\n"
"\n"
"================================= FAILURES "
"=================================\n"
"_______________________________ test_answer "
"________________________________\n"
"\n"
"    def test_answer():\n"
">       assert func(3) == 5\n"
"E       assert 4 == 5\n"
"E        +  where 4 = func(3)\n"
"\n"
"test_sample.py:6: AssertionError\n"
"========================= short test summary info "
"==========================\n"
"FAILED test_sample.py::test_answer - assert 4 == 5\n"
"============================ 1 failed in 0.12s ============================="
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:106
msgid ""
"The ``[100%]`` refers to the overall progress of running all test cases. "
"After it finishes, pytest then shows a failure report because ``func(3)`` "
"does not return ``5``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:110
msgid ""
"You can use the ``assert`` statement to verify test expectations. "
"pytest’s :ref:`Advanced assertion introspection <python:assert>` will "
"intelligently report intermediate values of the assert expression so you can "
"avoid the many names :ref:`of JUnit legacy methods <testcase-objects>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:113
msgid "Run multiple tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:115
msgid ""
"``pytest`` will run all files of the form ``test_*.py`` or ``*_test.py`` in "
"the current directory and its subdirectories. More generally, it "
"follows :ref:`standard test discovery rules <test discovery>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:119
msgid "Assert that a certain exception is raised"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:121
msgid ""
"Use the :ref:`raises <assertraises>` helper to assert that some code raises "
"an exception:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:123
msgid ""
"# content of test_sysexit.py\n"
"import pytest\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def f():\n"
"    raise SystemExit(1)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def test_mytest():\n"
"    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):\n"
"        f()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:137
msgid "Execute the test function with “quiet” reporting mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:139
msgid ""
"$ pytest -q test_sysexit.py\n"
".                                                                    [100%]\n"
"1 passed in 0.12s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:147
msgid ""
"The ``-q/--quiet`` flag keeps the output brief in this and following "
"examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:149
msgid ""
"See :ref:`assertraises` for specifying more details about the expected "
"exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:152
msgid "Group multiple tests in a class"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:156
msgid ""
"Once you develop multiple tests, you may want to group them into a class. "
"pytest makes it easy to create a class containing more than one test:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:158
msgid ""
"# content of test_class.py\n"
"class TestClass:\n"
"    def test_one(self):\n"
"        x = \"this\"\n"
"        assert \"h\" in x\n"
"\n"
"    def test_two(self):\n"
"        x = \"hello\"\n"
"        assert hasattr(x, \"check\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:170
msgid ""
"``pytest`` discovers all tests following its :ref:`Conventions for Python "
"test discovery <test discovery>`, so it finds both ``test_`` prefixed "
"functions. There is no need to subclass anything, but make sure to prefix "
"your class with ``Test`` otherwise the class will be skipped. We can simply "
"run the module by passing its filename:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:172
msgid ""
"$ pytest -q test_class.py\n"
".F                                                                   [100%]\n"
"================================= FAILURES "
"=================================\n"
"____________________________ TestClass.test_two "
"____________________________\n"
"\n"
"self = <test_class.TestClass object at 0xdeadbeef0001>\n"
"\n"
"    def test_two(self):\n"
"        x = \"hello\"\n"
">       assert hasattr(x, \"check\")\n"
"E       AssertionError: assert False\n"
"E        +  where False = hasattr('hello', 'check')\n"
"\n"
"test_class.py:8: AssertionError\n"
"========================= short test summary info "
"==========================\n"
"FAILED test_class.py::TestClass::test_two - AssertionError: assert False\n"
"1 failed, 1 passed in 0.12s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:192
msgid ""
"The first test passed and the second failed. You can easily see the "
"intermediate values in the assertion to help you understand the reason for "
"the failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:194
msgid "Grouping tests in classes can be beneficial for the following reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:196
msgid "Test organization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:197
msgid "Sharing fixtures for tests only in that particular class"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:198
msgid ""
"Applying marks at the class level and having them implicitly apply to all "
"tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:200
msgid ""
"Something to be aware of when grouping tests inside classes is that each "
"test has a unique instance of the class. Having each test share the same "
"class instance would be very detrimental to test isolation and would promote "
"poor test practices. This is outlined below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:206
msgid ""
"# content of test_class_demo.py\n"
"class TestClassDemoInstance:\n"
"    value = 0\n"
"\n"
"    def test_one(self):\n"
"        self.value = 1\n"
"        assert self.value == 1\n"
"\n"
"    def test_two(self):\n"
"        assert self.value == 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:220
msgid ""
"$ pytest -k TestClassDemoInstance -q\n"
".F                                                                   [100%]\n"
"================================= FAILURES "
"=================================\n"
"______________________ TestClassDemoInstance.test_two "
"______________________\n"
"\n"
"self = <test_class_demo.TestClassDemoInstance object at 0xdeadbeef0002>\n"
"\n"
"    def test_two(self):\n"
">       assert self.value == 1\n"
"E       assert 0 == 1\n"
"E        +  where 0 = <test_class_demo.TestClassDemoInstance object at "
"0xdeadbeef0002>.value\n"
"\n"
"test_class_demo.py:9: AssertionError\n"
"========================= short test summary info "
"==========================\n"
"FAILED test_class_demo.py::TestClassDemoInstance::test_two - assert 0 == 1\n"
"1 failed, 1 passed in 0.12s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:239
msgid ""
"Note that attributes added at class level are *class attributes*, so they "
"will be shared between tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:242
msgid "Compare floating-point values with pytest.approx"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:244
msgid ""
"``pytest`` also provides a number of utilities to make writing tests easier. "
"For example, you can use :func:`pytest.approx` to compare floating-point "
"values that may have small rounding errors:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:248
msgid ""
"# content of test_approx.py\n"
"import pytest\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def test_sum():\n"
"    assert (0.1 + 0.2) == pytest.approx(0.3)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:257
msgid ""
"This avoids the need for manual tolerance checks or using ``math.isclose`` "
"and works with scalars, lists, and NumPy arrays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:262
msgid "Request a unique temporary directory for functional tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:264
msgid ""
"``pytest`` provides :std:doc:`Builtin fixtures/function arguments <builtin>` "
"to request arbitrary resources, like a unique temporary directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:266
msgid ""
"# content of test_tmp_path.py\n"
"def test_needsfiles(tmp_path):\n"
"    print(tmp_path)\n"
"    assert 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:273
msgid ""
"List the name ``tmp_path`` in the test function signature and ``pytest`` "
"will lookup and call a fixture factory to create the resource before "
"performing the test function call. Before the test runs, ``pytest`` creates "
"a unique-per-test-invocation temporary directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:275
msgid ""
"$ pytest -q test_tmp_path.py\n"
"F                                                                    [100%]\n"
"================================= FAILURES "
"=================================\n"
"_____________________________ test_needsfiles "
"______________________________\n"
"\n"
"tmp_path = PosixPath('PYTEST_TMPDIR/test_needsfiles0')\n"
"\n"
"    def test_needsfiles(tmp_path):\n"
"        print(tmp_path)\n"
">       assert 0\n"
"E       assert 0\n"
"\n"
"test_tmp_path.py:3: AssertionError\n"
"--------------------------- Captured stdout call "
"---------------------------\n"
"PYTEST_TMPDIR/test_needsfiles0\n"
"========================= short test summary info "
"==========================\n"
"FAILED test_tmp_path.py::test_needsfiles - assert 0\n"
"1 failed in 0.12s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../doc/en/getting-started.rst:296
msgid ""
"More info on temporary directory handling is available at :ref:`Temporary "
"directories and files <tmp_path handling>`."
msgstr ""

